column26:
Replace the existing unstructured logging setup in all 6 services with structured JSON output using {{python-json-logger}}, centralised through a shared configuration module.

*Current state per service:*

* {{auth-service}}: {{logging.getLogger("auth-service")}} declared but never called
* {{election-service}}: no logger declared at all
* {{voting-service}}: no logger declared at all
* {{results-service}}: {{logging.getLogger("results-service")}} declared but never called
* {{voter-service}}: logger used in 2 places (email failures only)
* {{frontend-service}}: no logger declared at all

----

*Step 1 — Create* {{shared/logging_config.py}}

Create a new file at {{shared/logging_config.py}}:

{noformat}import logging
import logging.config

LOGGING = {
    'version': 1,
    'disable_existing_loggers': False,
    'formatters': {
        'json': {
            'format': '%(asctime)s %(name)s %(levelname)s %(message)s',
            'datefmt': '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ',
            'class': 'pythonjsonlogger.jsonlogger.JsonFormatter',
        }
    },
    'handlers': {
        'stdout': {
            'class': 'logging.StreamHandler',
            'stream': 'ext://sys.stdout',
            'formatter': 'json',
        }
    },
    'loggers': {
        '': {
            'handlers': ['stdout'],
            'level': 'INFO',
        }
    },
}

def configure_logging():
    logging.config.dictConfig(LOGGING)
{noformat}

----

*Step 2 — Update each service* {{app.py}}

In every {{app.py}}, remove any existing inline logging setup and replace with:

{noformat}import logging
from logging_config import configure_logging

configure_logging()
logger = logging.getLogger('service-name')
{noformat}

{quote}Note: import as {{from logging_config import configure_logging}} (not {{from shared.logging_config}}) since {{shared/}} is added directly to {{sys.path}} by each service's path-resolution block. {{frontend-service}} requires the {{sys.path}} block to be added as it currently has none.{quote}

Logger names: {{auth-service}}, {{election-service}}, {{voting-service}}, {{results-service}}, {{voter-service}}, {{frontend-service}}.

----

*Step 3 — Add structured log calls*

Use {{%}}-style formatting (not f-strings) throughout.

* *INFO* at the entry point of every route handler:

{noformat}logger.info('Request received: %s %s', request.method, request.url.path)
{noformat}

All JSON API route handlers that do not already accept {{request: Request}} must have it added as a parameter.

* *WARNING* on auth failure or invalid token (401/403 rejections):

{noformat}logger.warning('Auth failure: %s', reason)
{noformat}

Apply in: {{auth-service}} ({{login}}, {{verify_token}}), {{voter-service}} ({{verify_identity}} DOB mismatch), {{frontend-service}} ({{login}}).

* *ERROR* on caught DB errors — replace raw {{str(e)}} returned to the client with:

{noformat}logger.error('Database error in %s: %s', function_name, e)
{noformat}

Return a generic {{"Internal server error"}} message to the client. Apply in: {{auth-service}} {{register()}} and {{voter-service}} {{add_voter()}}.

* *ERROR* on failed external {{httpx}} calls in {{voting-service}} and {{frontend-service}} (currently unhandled):

{noformat}logger.error('External service call failed: %s %s — %s', method, url, e)
{noformat}

Return a service unavailable response after logging. Do not interpolate token values into the URL string in log messages.

----

*Constraints*

* Do not log any sensitive values — no passwords, tokens, or voter identifiers.
* Do not change any business logic, route behaviour, or response structures beyond what is described above.
* Only modify {{shared/logging_config.py}} and the six {{app.py}} files.

voter-service/app.py returns the wrong service identifier in its health endpoint.

Current response (voter-service/app.py line ~81):
  {"status": "healthy", "service": "voter"}

This service is deployed as admin-service in the cluster
(admin-deployment.yaml, label: app: admin-service). Any health check
asserting the deployed service name will fail.

Fix: change the health response to match the deployment identity:
  {"status": "healthy", "service": "admin"}

Note: frontend-service has NO /health endpoint at all. The deploy script
cannot health-check it. Add a basic health endpoint to frontend-service/app.py:

  @app.get("/health")
  async def health():
      return {"status": "healthy", "service": "frontend"}

test_api.py currently has no health check stage. The first assertion is an
auth registration call. Add a Stage 0 health check that runs before all other
stages and fails fast if any service is unreachable.

Expected responses per service:
  auth-service     GET /health → {"status": "healthy", "service": "auth"}
  election-service GET /health → {"status": "healthy", "service": "election"}
  voting-service   GET /health → {"status": "healthy", "service": "voting"}
  results-service  GET /health → {"status": "healthy", "service": "results"}
  voter-service    GET /health → {"status": "healthy", "service": "admin"}
                   (returns "admin" after fix in preceding ticket)
  frontend-service GET /health → {"status": "healthy", "service": "frontend"}
                   (endpoint does not exist yet — depends on preceding ticket)

The health check stage should assert both status == "healthy" AND the correct
service name field. The current audit found these are shallow checks with no
DB connectivity probe — that is acceptable for now.
